ociate with prolonged PFS in anti-PD-1 treated solid tumors, while serum butyrate/propionate show negative correlation with anti-CTLA-4 efficacy. Mechanisms involve HDAC inhibition, GPCR agonism, and A2aR signaling, but these are largely derived from preclinical models or indirect clinical correlations.\n    - Conclude with the distinction/gap: Thus, while systemic metabolite levels and microbial origins are established, direct intra-tumoral quantification and paired tissue-systemic correlation required to answer the specific question remain absent in the current evidence base.\n\n5.  **Determine Decision & Missing Info:**\n    - `decision`: `external_research` (Core requirement of absolute TME concentrations is unmet. Need new studies specifically measuring intra-tumoral metabolites via targeted metabolomics/microdialysis in paired human samples.)\n    - `still_missing`: Absolute physiological concentrations (e.g., μM, ng/mL) of inosine and specific SCFAs directly quantified within human tumour tissue biopsies, along with paired serum/stool measurements and their statistical correlation with ICI response metrics (ORR, PFS, OS).\n    - `read_focus`: Targeted metabolomics studies focusing on human solid tumor biopsy samples from ICI-treated cohorts; studies utilizing intratumoral microdialysis or spatial metabolomics to map local metabolite concentrations.\n    - `external_ask`: Retrieve prospective human clinical studies or translational cohorts that perform LC-MS/MS or similar targeted metabolomics on fresh-frozen or FFPE human tumor tissue biopsies (and matched serum/stool) from patients undergoing ICI therapy, specifically reporting absolute intratumoral concentrations of inosine and short-chain fatty acids (butyrate, propionate, acetate, valerate) and their correlation with treatment response.\n    - `reason`: The provided materials consistently state that while plasma/serum/fecal associations and preclinical mechanisms are documented, none report absolute quant